**Currency Update:** Add both autoHighlight and autoSelect.
```jsx
{/* Currency */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 1.5, lg: 2 }}>
  <Autocomplete
    disableClearable
    autoHighlight // <-- ADDED
    autoSelect    // <-- ADDED
    options={currencyOptions}
    value={row.currency || null}
    onChange={(event, newValue) => {
      handleRowChange(row.id, "currency", newValue || "");
      handleRowChange(row.id, "amount", "");
    }}
    filterOptions={(options, params) => {
      const { inputValue } = params;
      if (inputValue.trim().length === 0) return [];
      return options.filter((option) =>
        option.toLowerCase().includes(inputValue.toLowerCase())
      );
    }}
    noOptionsText="Type to search..."
    slotProps={{
      popper: { modifiers: [{ name: 'flip', enabled: true }, { name: 'preventOverflow', enabled: true }] }
    }}
    renderInput={(params) => (
      <TextField
        {...params}
        label="Currency *"
        placeholder={!row.currency ? "Search..." : ""}
        InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }}
        inputProps={{
          ...params.inputProps,
          maxLength: 3,
          style: { textTransform: 'uppercase' }
        }}
      />
    )}
  />
</Grid>

```
### 3. Txn Type (D for Debit, C for Credit)
Add an onKeyDown event to the Txn Type TextField to intercept keystrokes. If the user types "d" or "c" while focused on the field, it will instantly set the state and ignore other keystrokes.
```jsx
{/* Txn Type */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 1, lg: 1.5 }}>
  <TextField
    select 
    label="Type *" 
    value={row.txnType} 
    fullWidth 
    InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }} 
    onChange={(e) => handleRowChange(row.id, "txnType", e.target.value)} 
    onKeyDown={(e) => {
      // Intercept keystrokes to auto-select
      const key = e.key.toLowerCase();
      if (key === 'd') {
        e.preventDefault(); // Prevents standard browser behavior
        handleRowChange(row.id, "txnType", "Debit");
      } else if (key === 'c') {
        e.preventDefault();
        handleRowChange(row.id, "txnType", "Credit");
      }
    }}
    SelectProps={{ 
      displayEmpty: true, 
      renderValue: (selected) => !selected ? <span style={{ color: 'gray', opacity: 0.8 }}>Type</span> : selected 
    }}
  >
    <MenuItem value="Debit">Debit</MenuItem>
    <MenuItem value="Credit">Credit</MenuItem>
  </TextField>
</Grid>

```
### 4. Amount Field Validation (Red on 0)
You can compute if the amount is exactly zero using parseFloat and trigger MUI's native error state, which automatically turns the border, label, and helper text red.
```jsx
{/* Amount */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 2, lg: 2.8 }}>
  <TextField
    label="Amount *" 
    type="text" 
    value={row.amount} 
    InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }}
    fullWidth 
    placeholder="0.0000" 
    inputProps={{ style: { textAlign: "right" } }}
    // Checks if input is not empty AND equals zero numerically
    error={row.amount !== "" && parseFloat(row.amount) === 0} 
    onChange={(e) => {
      const rawVal = e.target.value;
      if (/[^0-9.]/.test(rawVal)) {
        showSnackBar("Only numbers and decimal point are allowed.", "warning"); return;
      }
      if (rawVal === "" || /^\d{0,16}(\.\d{0,4})?$/.test(rawVal)) { 
        handleRowChange(row.id, "amount", rawVal); 
      }
      else { 
        showSnackBar("Exceeded maximum digits or decimal places.", "warning"); 
      }
    }}
  />
  <Typography 
    variant="caption" 
    // Turns the caption red if the error state is triggered
    sx={{ ...jpStyles.captionText, color: (row.amount !== "" && parseFloat(row.amount) === 0) ? 'error.main' : 'text.secondary' }}
  >
    {(row.amount !== "" && parseFloat(row.amount) === 0) ? "Amount cannot be zero" : "Max 16 digits, 4 decimals"}
  </Typography>
</Grid>

```
